WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.352
Start by inspecting the source rows.

00:00:02.352 --> 00:00:06.027
Look for visible and invisible formatting differences.

00:00:06.027 --> 00:00:13.682
Notice missing emails or phones, blank values, inconsistent punctuation, and possible duplicate people.

00:00:13.682 --> 00:00:16.942
The purpose of this first query is not to fix anything.

00:00:16.942 --> 00:00:20.777
It is to build evidence about the source before defining rules.

00:00:20.777 --> 00:00:27.462
Connect this idea to a healthcare pipeline validating contact, demographic, and event fields.

00:00:27.462 --> 00:00:32.767
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:40.257
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:40.257 --> 00:00:46.052
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:46.052 --> 00:00:51.887
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
